iligence/","/ai-architect/","/uae/"]}-->
<section class="block">
<h2>Who this is for</h2>
<p>Most companies that call me are not short of engineers. They are short of someone who can sit with the CEO in the morning, review a pull request in the afternoon and explain the trade-off to the board the next day. That is the job of a CTO, and plenty of businesses need it long before they can justify, or attract, a full-time one.</p>
<p>You are probably a good fit if any of these sound familiar:</p>
<ul>
<li>You are a non-technical founder and every technical decision feels like a bet you cannot check.</li>
<li>Your engineering lead is excellent at shipping but has never set a multi-year technical strategy or reported to a board.</li>
<li>You have an AI initiative that impressed in a demo and has stalled since.</li>
<li>Investors are asking about scalability, security or AI, and the honest answers are vague.</li>
<li>Your last CTO left and you need steady leadership while you find the right replacement.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are still deciding whether you need this at all, I wrote a longer piece on <a href="/insights/when-do-you-need-a-fractional-cto/">when you actually need a fractional CTO</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>The problems I solve</h2>
<h3>Decisions that are expensive to reverse</h3>
<p>Choice of cloud, data model, build versus buy, which AI vendor to commit to. These decisions set your cost base and your speed for years. I make sure they are made deliberately, written down and tied to business outcomes rather than whatever was trendy that quarter.</p>
<h3>A team that has outgrown its structure</h3>
<p>Ten engineers can coordinate over coffee. Forty cannot. I design team topology, ownership boundaries and the engineering practices (reviews, decision records, on-call) that let more people ship without stepping on each other.</p>
<h3>AI ambition without a plan</h3>
<p>I help you pick the AI use cases that pay for themselves, ground them in your own data, and put evaluation and cost controls in place before you scale. My <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture work</a> covers the detail.</p>
<h3>Technology the board cannot see into</h3>
<p>I translate engineering reality into the language of risk, cost and timeline, so your board and investors get honest, useful reporting instead of reassurance.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>How I approach a fractional CTO engagement</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Listen first.</strong> In the first two weeks I talk to founders, engineers, product and key customers, and read the code and the cloud bill. Opinions come later.</li>
<li><strong>Write the current state down.</strong> A short, candid assessment of architecture, team, delivery and risk. Most teams have never seen their situation on one page.</li>
<li><strong>Agree a technical strategy.</strong> A roadmap tied to business milestones, with the few decisions that matter most called out and owned.</li>
<li><strong>Set up the operating rhythm.</strong> Architecture decision records, planning, metrics and a light governance process the team actually uses.</li>
<li><strong>Hire and grow people.</strong> I help define roles, interview senior candidates and mentor the engineers who will lead after me.</li>
<li><strong>Hand over cleanly.</strong> The goal is a team that runs without me, or a permanent CTO who inherits a clear picture rather than a mess.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>What you get</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Deliverable</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Technical assessment</td><td>An honest view of architecture, team, security and delivery risk, in plain language.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Technology strategy and roadmap</td><td>Priorities tied to revenue, cost and fundraising milestones.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Architecture decision records</td><td>A written trail of why things were built the way they were.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hiring plan and role definitions</td><td>The right senior hires, in the right order, with a clear bar.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Board and investor reporting</td><td>Technology risk and progress your board can act on.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Due diligence readiness</td><td>Answers ready before an investor asks. See <a href="/technical-due-diligence/">technical due diligence</a>.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>How an engagement runs</h2>
<p>We start with a free 30-minute call to see whether there is a fit. If there is, most clients begin with a two to four week architecture and AI readiness review. It produces a written assessment and a prioritised roadmap, and it is useful whether or not we continue.</p>
<p>From there, the fractional CTO role usually runs at one to two days a week, with a regular cadence: a weekly session with the leadership team, time embedded with engineering, and a monthly written update. The commitment flexes up around fundraises, launches and senior hires, and down once the team is running well.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Not sure where to start? Run the <a href="/#assess">two-minute challenge analyser</a> and bring the result to the intro call.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Fractional, interim or full-time?</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Best when</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fractional CTO</td><td>You need senior judgement now, but not five days a week</td><td>Needs a strong engineering lead day to day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interim CTO</td><td>A full-time seat is empty and must be covered for a defined period</td><td>Higher cost; focus can drift to firefighting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full-time CTO</td><td>Technology is the business and the scale justifies it</td><td>Slow and expensive to hire, costly to get wrong</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Many of my clients use a fractional engagement to bridge the gap: getting the strategy and team right first, then hiring a permanent CTO who walks into a clear plan.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Relevant experience</h2>
<p>I am a principal engineer by background, and I still build. That matters, because a CTO who cannot read the code ends up managing by rumour.</p>
<ul>
<li>I have designed and scaled event-driven systems handling more than a million transactions a day.</li>
<li>I founded and built <strong>Wisp</strong>, an open-source platform that deploys AI-generated apps to your own Kubernetes cluster. I have made the founder trade-offs you are making now.</li>
<li>I work across the stack, from <a href="/ontology-knowledge-graph/">ontology and knowledge graph design</a> to Terraform on AWS, Azure and GCP.</li>
<li>I bring domain depth in <a href="/healthcare-data-architecture/">healthcare data</a>, including OMOP CDM and OHDSI, and co-authored peer-reviewed research in Science Immunology.</li>
<li>I am a MongoDB Community Leader and spend time mentoring engineers in public.</li>
</ul>
<p>I am based in the United States and the UAE and work with teams in both time zones. If you are in the Gulf, see <a href="/uae/">fractional CTO in Dubai and Abu Dhabi</a>. You can also read <a href="/#work">case studies</a> describing the kinds of problems I am brought in to solve.</p>
</section>
